---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../../components/navigation/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { buildLocalePath } from '../../../../i18n/utils';

const locale = 'en-US' as const;

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BlogPosting",
  "headline": "How to Convert Your Hourly Wage to an Annual Salary",
  "description": "Learn how to convert an hourly wage to an annual salary, with the simple formula, a worked example, and the details to check before you compare job offers.",
  "datePublished": "2026-11-03",
  "dateModified": "2026-11-03",
  "author": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "PayRenTax"
  },
  "publisher": {
    "@type": "Organization",
    "name": "PayRenTax",
    "logo": {
      "@type": "ImageObject",
      "url": "https://payrentax.me/logo.svg"
    }
  },
  "inLanguage": "en-US",
  "keywords": "hourly to salary, convert hourly wage to annual salary, hourly wage calculator"
};
---

<BaseLayout
  locale={locale}
  title="How to Convert Your Hourly Wage to an Annual Salary | PayRenTax"
  description="Learn how to convert an hourly wage to an annual salary, with the simple formula, a worked example, and the details to check before you compare job offers."
  canonical="/en-us/blog/hourly-to-salary-conversion-2026/"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  </Fragment>

  <Breadcrumbs
    locale={locale}
    trail=[
      { label: 'Blog', href: buildLocalePath(locale, 'blog') },
      { label: 'How to Convert Your Hourly Wage to an Annual Salary', href: buildLocalePath(locale, 'blog/hourly-to-salary-conversion-2026') }
    ]
  />
<article class="article">
    <header class="article-header">
      <p class="eyebrow">PayRenTax Blog · Salary Guides</p>
      <h1>How to Convert Your Hourly Wage to an Annual Salary</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        An hourly wage can be hard to compare with a salaried offer. This guide shows the simple conversion,
        works through an example, and explains the details that can change the result.
      </p>
      <p class="article-meta">
        <time datetime="2026-11-03">November 3, 2026</time> · 5 min read · By PayRenTax
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="article-section">
      <h2>How the Conversion Works</h2>
      <p>
        To convert an hourly wage to an annual salary, multiply your hourly rate by the number of hours
        you work each week, then multiply that weekly amount by 52 weeks. In short:
      </p>
      <p><strong>Annual salary = hourly wage × hours per week × 52</strong></p>
      <p>
        For a full-time schedule of 40 hours per week, the calculation is hourly wage × 2,080 hours,
        because 40 × 52 = 2,080. If you work a different number of hours each week, use your own
        usual weekly hours instead of 40. You can check your result with the
        <a href="/en-us/salary/hourly-to-salary/">hourly to salary calculator</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section">
      <h2>Work Out Your Weekly, Monthly, and Annual Pay</h2>
      <p>
        Start with your hourly wage and your usual hours per week. Multiply them to get your weekly pay,
        then use that figure for the longer periods:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Weekly pay:</strong> hourly wage × hours per week</li>
        <li><strong>Monthly pay:</strong> annual salary ÷ 12</li>
        <li><strong>Annual salary:</strong> hourly wage × hours per week × 52</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Dividing the annual figure by 12 gives a monthly average. It is an average because months have
        different numbers of weeks, so individual monthly paychecks may vary if you are paid weekly
        or biweekly. For a closer look at what actually lands in your paycheck, see our
        <a href="/en-us/salary/paycheck-calculator/">paycheck calculator</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section">
      <h2>A Worked Example</h2>
      <p>
        Suppose you earn $25 per hour and usually work 40 hours per week. The conversion is:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Weekly pay: $25 × 40 = $1,000</li>
        <li>Annual salary: $1,000 × 52 = $52,000</li>
        <li>Monthly average: $52,000 ÷ 12 = $4,333.33</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If instead you earn $20 per hour and work 35 hours per week, the result is $20 × 35 × 52 =
        $36,400 per year. The same formula works in the other direction too: our
        <a href="/en-us/salary/salary-to-hourly/">salary to hourly calculator</a> converts an annual
        salary back to an hourly rate.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section">
      <h2>What to Watch Out For</h2>
      <p>
        The simple formula assumes a steady schedule all year. Check these points before you compare
        two offers or plan a budget around the annual figure:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Variable hours:</strong> if your hours change from week to week, use a typical week, and treat the result as an estimate.</li>
        <li><strong>Time off:</strong> unpaid time off reduces the total you actually earn in a year.</li>
        <li><strong>Overtime:</strong> hours beyond your usual schedule may be paid differently; see our <a href="/en-us/salary/overtime-tax-calculator/">overtime calculator</a> for a separate check.</li>
        <li><strong>Gross vs. take-home pay:</strong> the converted figure is gross pay, before any deductions. It is not the amount you take home.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If any of these apply, run the calculation with your actual hours and pay details rather than
        relying on a single headline annual figure.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="article-section">
      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <h3>How do I convert $30 per hour to a salary?</h3>
      <p>
        At 40 hours per week, $30 × 40 × 52 = $62,400 per year. At a different weekly schedule,
        replace 40 with your own usual hours per week.
      </p>
      <h3>Is the annual figure my take-home pay?</h3>
      <p>
        No. The conversion gives gross annual pay, before deductions. Use the
        <a href="/en-us/salary/paycheck-calculator/">paycheck calculator</a> to explore what a paycheck may look like after deductions.
      </p>
      <h3>What if I work part-time?</h3>
      <p>
        The same formula applies. Multiply your hourly wage by your usual part-time hours per week,
        then by 52, to get the annual equivalent for that schedule.
      </p>
      <p>
        Try the <a href="/en-us/salary/hourly-to-salary/">hourly to salary calculator</a> to see your
        weekly, monthly, and annual pay in one place.
      </p>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article { max-width: 760px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem 1rem 4rem; }
  .article-header { margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .eyebrow { text-transform: uppercase; letter-spacing: 0.08em; font-size: 0.85rem; color: #64748b; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; }
  .article h1 { font-size: 2.25rem; line-height: 1.15; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.2rem; line-height: 1.6; color: #334155; }
  .article-meta { color: #64748b; font-size: 0.95rem; }
  .article-section { margin-bottom: 2rem; }
  .article-section h2 { font-size: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  .article-section h3 { font-size: 1.15rem; margin: 1.25rem 0 0.5rem; }
  .article-section p, .article-section li { line-height: 1.7; color: #1e293b; }
</style>
